{
  "summary": "Critiqued the proposal on five fronts: (1) The automatic summarization agent triggered at done-time is the riskiest part — it's an unbounded LLM call that can silently corrupt accumulated context if it hallucinates or over-condenses, and there's no rollback mechanism; a simpler append-only design with manual curation would be safer and more predictable. (2) The 50KB soft cap with automatic summarization creates a hidden quality cliff — context quality degrades non-deterministically when the cap is hit, and users won't know why agents suddenly make worse decisions; a hard cap with a visible warning and manual trim prompt is more honest. (3) Hooking into three separate done-transition points (tui.py x2, server.py x1) to trigger an agent call creates tight coupling and fragility — if a fourth transition path is added later (e.g., CLI, API, or a new UI), the context update will silently be skipped; instead, the context-append logic should be a single function called from move_to_stage('done') in state.py or from the existing done-script mechanism, which already runs async and has error handling. (4) The proposal doesn't address what happens when the extraction agent produces low-quality or wrong summaries — garbage entries will accumulate and pollute all future feature work on that board, with no quality gate or human review step before context is committed. (5) Using an LLM call to extract lessons at done-time is expensive and slow for what could be a simpler approach: have the implementation agent write a 'lessons_learned' field into the feature JSON during implementation (when context is fresh), then just append that field's content to BOARD_CONTEXT.md at done-time with no additional agent call needed."
